            <ArticleTitle>[Splenic torsion as cause of acute abdomen in children].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>587-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A six-year-old boy was admitted with symptoms consistent with acute appendicitis. Immediately before placement of the first trocar, a large abdominal mass was observed which on imaging was identified as a torsioned spleen. Due to suboptimal reperfusion and risk of reperfusion-mediated morbidity a splenectomy was performed. The recovery was uneventful. The child had a twin brother who on a routine ultrasound examination was found to have a normally positioned spleen. Challenges in diagnosis and treatment of children with splenic torsion is discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
